{
  "title": "What are they like?",
  "title_target": "אֵיךְ הוּא?",
  "goal": "Describe people and things with adjectives, and master the one rule that governs the rest of Hebrew: agreement.",
  "bekal": [
    "describe people, places, and things",
    "make adjectives agree with nouns in all four forms",
    "tell \"a big house\" from \"the house is big\""
  ],
  "dialogs": [
    {
      "title": "שִׂיחָה — דִּירָה חֲדָשָׁה",
      "intro": "Budi is looking for a room to rent. He calls Dana, who has advertised her room.",
      "lines": [
        {
          "speaker": "בּוּדִי:",
          "text": "שָׁלוֹם, אֲנִי מְחַפֵּשׂ חֶדֶר.",
          "baca": "shalom, ani mekhapes kheder.",
          "id": "Hello, I'm looking for a room.",
          "literal": "hello, I look-for room."
        },
        {
          "speaker": "דָּנָה:",
          "text": "יֵשׁ לִי חֶדֶר גָּדוֹל וּמוּאָר.",
          "baca": "yesh li kheder gadol umu'ar.",
          "id": "I have a big, bright room.",
          "literal": "there-is to-me room big and-bright."
        },
        {
          "speaker": "בּוּדִי:",
          "text": "וְהַדִּירָה? הִיא גְּדוֹלָה?",
          "baca": "vehadirah? hi gdolah?",
          "id": "And the apartment? Is it big?",
          "literal": "and-the-apartment? she big (f)?"
        },
        {
          "speaker": "דָּנָה:",
          "text": "לֹא גְּדוֹלָה, אֲבָל נְעִימָה וּשְׁקֵטָה.",
          "baca": "lo gdolah, aval ne'imah ushketah.",
          "id": "Not big, but pleasant and quiet.",
          "literal": "not big (f), but pleasant (f) and-quiet (f)."
        },
        {
          "speaker": "בּוּדִי:",
          "text": "וְהַשְּׁכֵנִים? הֵם נְחְמָדִים?",
          "baca": "vehashkhenim? hem nekhmadim?",
          "id": "And the neighbors? Are they nice?",
          "literal": "and-the-neighbors? they nice (m pl)?"
        },
        {
          "speaker": "דָּנָה:",
          "text": "מְאוֹד נְחְמָדִים. שְׁתֵּי סְטוּדֶנְטִיּוֹת שְׁקֵטוֹת.",
          "baca": "me'od nekhmadim. shtei studentiyot shketot.",
          "id": "Very nice. Two quiet students.",
          "literal": "very nice (m pl). two students (f) quiet (f pl)."
        },
        {
          "speaker": "בּוּדִי:",
          "text": "הַחֶדֶר יָקָר?",
          "baca": "hakheder yakar?",
          "id": "Is the room expensive?",
          "literal": "the-room expensive?"
        },
        {
          "speaker": "דָּנָה:",
          "text": "לֹא יָקָר. אַלְפַּיִם שֶׁקֶל לְחֹדֶשׁ.",
          "baca": "lo yakar. alpayim shekel lekhodesh.",
          "id": "Not expensive. Two thousand shekels a month.",
          "literal": "not expensive. two-thousand shekel for-month."
        },
        {
          "speaker": "בּוּדִי:",
          "text": "זֶה מְחִיר טוֹב. וְהַשְּׁכוּנָה?",
          "baca": "zeh mekhir tov. vehashkhunah?",
          "id": "That's a good price. And the neighborhood?",
          "literal": "this price good. and-the-neighborhood?"
        },
        {
          "speaker": "דָּנָה:",
          "text": "שְׁכוּנָה יְשָׁנָה אֲבָל יָפָה מְאוֹד.",
          "baca": "shkhunah yeshanah aval yafah me'od.",
          "id": "An old neighborhood, but very pretty.",
          "literal": "neighborhood old (f) but pretty (f) very."
        },
        {
          "speaker": "בּוּדִי:",
          "text": "מְצֻיָּן. מָתַי אֶפְשָׁר לִרְאוֹת?",
          "baca": "metsuyan. matai efshar lir'ot?",
          "id": "Great. When can I see it?",
          "literal": "excellent. when possible to-see?"
        },
        {
          "speaker": "דָּנָה:",
          "text": "מָחָר בָּעֶרֶב. אַתָּה חָפְשִׁי?",
          "baca": "makhar ba'erev. atah khofshi?",
          "id": "Tomorrow evening. Are you free?",
          "literal": "tomorrow in-the-evening. you free?"
        }
      ],
      "notes": [
        "⚠️ **The adjective comes AFTER the noun** — the reverse of English: חֶדֶר גָּדוֹל = \"room big\", a big room. Your English reflex will push the adjective to the front; train yourself to say the thing first, then describe it.",
        "⚠️ **Adjectives agree with the noun in TWO ways at once: gender and number.** חֶדֶר גָּדוֹל (a big room) but דִּירָה גְּדוֹלָה (a big apartment). In English \"big\" is always \"big\", whatever the noun — so this is the new habit that takes longest to build.",
        "**Chains of adjectives all agree.** נְעִימָה וּשְׁקֵטָה — two adjectives, both feminine, because דִּירָה is feminine. If you forget to adjust even one, it jars on the ear.",
        "⚠️ **\"A big house\" vs \"the house is big\".** The only difference is one הַ־ on the adjective: הַבַּיִת הַגָּדוֹל = \"the big house\" (naming a thing); הַבַּיִת גָּדוֹל = \"the house is big\" (a full sentence that says something). One הַ־ decides whether you're naming a thing or saying something about it — hardly any other A1 rule matters as much.",
        "**מְאוֹד** (*me'od*, \"very\") comes **after** the adjective: יָפָה מְאוֹד, \"pretty very\". The opposite of English. מְאוֹד יָפָה is also heard, but it's more emphatic.",
        "**הֵם / הֵן** — \"they\" also has two forms: הֵם for groups with any men, הֵן for all-female groups. A hundred women and one man still make הֵם. The same rule applies to אַתֶּם/אַתֶּן.",
        "**אֶפְשָׁר** (*efshar*) = \"it's possible, may I, can I\" — a word that never changes form and is followed directly by the infinitive: אֶפְשָׁר לִרְאוֹת? (can I see?), אֶפְשָׁר לְשַׁלֵּם? (can I pay?). The most economical polite sentence in Hebrew.",
        "**מָחָר בָּעֶרֶב** — notice בָּ (the merger of בְּ + הַ). \"In the evening\", i.e. tomorrow evening. Telling time in full comes in unit 9."
      ],
